{"qid":"Q1191125","names":["Moghulistan","Eastern Chagatai Khanate","Eastern Moghulistan","Western Moghulistan"],"title":"Moghulistan","endonym":{"native":"مغولستان","lang":"fa"},"capsule":"Moghulistan (the Moghul or Eastern Chagatai Khanate) was a Turco-Mongol khanate formed in the 1340s when the Chagatai Khanate fragmented, comprising the steppe and semi-arid zones north of the Tian Shan in present-day eastern Kazakhstan, Kyrgyzstan, and northwest Xinjiang. Real power in the khanate often rested with the Dughlat tribal lords while the Chinggisid khans served as figureheads; the rulers faced perpetual pressure from Timur and his successors in the west and Ming China to the east. By the late 15th century, the khanate had contracted to the Tarim region; around 1514 Said Khan established the successor Yarkent Khanate, marking the effective end of Moghulistan as a distinct political unit.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Moghulistan's territorial extent is reconstructed from Timurid chronicles, Chinese dynastic histories, and later Chagatai-language sources; the steppe frontiers were fluid and seasonal, and the polity's boundaries are broadly approximate rather than administratively demarcated.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Moghulistan","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moghulistan"}],"britannica":"topic/Mughulistan","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Moghulistan","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Tughlugh Timur Khan","nativeName":"تغلق تيمور خان","roman":"Tughluq Tīmūr Khān","start":1347,"end":1363,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Ilyas Khoja","nativeName":"ال